<.shell current={:qr_scanner}>
  <h1>QR scanner</h1>
  <p class="lead">Scan ID cards with the phone's camera.</p>

  <div class="example measure">
    <.qr_scanner id="sample-scanner" label="Scan ID cards" variant={:success} />
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <p>
    A page used on a phone to read QR codes, such as checking an ID card or taking attendance.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      One big button starts the camera. Its picture is 4:3 on a phone and a square from an
      iPad up, never taller than 45% of the screen, so Stop scanning stays in sight.
    </li>
    <li>
      A QR reader draws a square to aim at and reads only around it. A hint under the square
      says what to hold there.
    </li>
    <li>The screen stays on while it scans, so an iPad at the door does not sleep.</li>
    <li>Stop scanning puts the button back.</li>
    <li>
      Switch camera, shown when the device has more than one, swaps the back and front cameras,
      as for an iPad at the door facing the members. The device remembers it.
    </li>
    <li>
      The Sound switch is a <a href={page_path(:forms) <> "#switch"}>compact switch</a>
      under the button. The phone remembers it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each read goes to the page, which answers with a <a href={page_path(:band)}>band</a>.
      A page that scans one card after another, like Take attendance, draws each answer on
      the picture instead: the member's name in large type, green, blue, or red, for 3 seconds.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.qr_scanner label="Scan ID cards" /&gt;</code>. The page handles <code>"scanned"</code>.
    <code>data-continuous</code>
    keeps scanning after a read. <code>reader={:barcode}</code>
    reads equipment barcodes, such as Code 128 and UPC, as well as QR codes, with no aim
    square. <code>hint</code>
    goes under the square, and a <code>:result</code>
    slot, with a <code>kind</code>
    of <code>:success</code>, <code>:info</code>, or <code>:danger</code>, draws over the picture while it scans.
  </p>
</.shell>
